# CSPRNG — fills `n` bytes at `buf` with kernel-supplied randomness.
# Audit 2026-04-26 F-001: removed xorshift state and the deterministic
# fallback constant. UUIDs now draw fresh entropy per call.
fn _stik_fill_random(buf, n) {
    # getrandom(2), the only source. Preferred over opening /dev/urandom
    # because it works in sandboxed envs that block file IO and never
    # returns short reads for buffers <= 256 B.
    #
    # F-022 (audit 2026-09-23): reached through the stdlib's
    # sys_getrandom, never a raw syscall number. Each target's syscall
    # peer supplies it — getrandom 318 on x86_64-linux, 278 on
    # aarch64-linux, #45 on agnos, ProcessPrng on Windows, getentropy
    # (normalised to the byte count) on macOS. The raw 318 this replaced
    # is getrandom only on x86_64-linux: aarch64 returned -ENOSYS on every
    # call, so the fast path never ran there, and agnos has no syscall 318.
    # Spelling it SYS_GETRANDOM instead would not compile for Windows,
    # whose peer defines no such constant.
    var got = sys_getrandom(buf, n, 0);
    if (got == n) { return 0; }
    # F-023 (audit 2026-09-23): there is no /dev/urandom fallback. The
    # one this replaced opened, read and closed by raw x86_64-linux
    # numbers (2 / 0 / 3), which on agnos are getpid / exit / spawn, and
    # neither agnos nor Windows has a /dev/urandom to open. sys_getrandom
    # covers every target, so the fallback only served Linux < 3.17 and
    # sandboxes that deny getrandom but still allow the open.
    #
    # Identifier generation is security-critical (agent_id, session_id,
    # trace_id back authorisation in aegis/sigil), so we fail loudly
    # rather than degrade to a deterministic seed. sys_write / sys_exit,
    # not raw numbers: raw 60 is winsize on agnos, whose exit is #0.
    # F-012 (audit 2026-05-10): the byte-count was hardcoded 67 for a
    # 68-byte literal; the trailing newline silently dropped. Use
    # strlen() so the count tracks the literal.
    var msg = "agnostik: CSPRNG unavailable (getrandom failed)\n";
    sys_write(2, msg, strlen(msg));
    sys_exit(70);
    return 0 - 1;
}
